Subject: FIX: wayland backend: commit the surface when the pointer lock is taken or dropped

togglePointerLock (Right Ctrl upstream, Ctrl+Alt+G with 0045) creates or destroys a zwp_locked_pointer_v1
on the output's main surface. Its region is double-buffered surface state, and a host compositor applies the
lock - and, in KWin, its removal - at the surface's next wl_surface.commit (KWin's pointerconstraints_v1.cpp
sets and resets the pending pointerLockRegion). The output commits only when it presents a frame, and an
idle nested session presents none (pointer motion moves the cursor surface only), so the grab took effect
whenever something next repainted, and so did its release: pressed on a quiet desktop, nothing happened.

Measured 2026-10-14 on host KWin 6.7.91 (bench/ab/vmview_grab_test_1014.sh VMVIEW=0 with
bench/tools/host_pointer_constraints.bt): after Ctrl+Alt+G the lock request arrives but stays inactive and
the host cursor still moves 284 px for a 200-count relative move; after a guest repaint the lock is active
and the cursor holds; after the second Ctrl+Alt+G it stays locked until the next repaint. With this change
the main surface is committed right after the lock is created or destroyed (between frames the layers have
no cached state, so the commit applies nothing else).

(An earlier 0051 - layers without input - rested on a wrong reading and is withdrawn: the main-surface lock
does activate once committed.)

From the research (virtio-gpu-desktop patches/kwin, 0051), applies to 6.7.5
as it is.

diff --git a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
index 42b2d82..3dade69 100644
--- a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
+++ b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
@@ -578,6 +578,8 @@ void WaylandOutput::lockPointer(Pointer *pointer, bool lock)
         const bool surfaceWasLocked = m_pointerLock && m_hasPointerLock;
         m_pointerLock.reset();
         m_hasPointerLock = false;
+        // the lock's end is surface state too: committed now, not at the next frame (an idle session has none)
+        m_surface->commit(KWayland::Client::Surface::CommitFlag::None);
         if (surfaceWasLocked) {
             inhibitShortcuts(false);
             updateWindowTitle();
@@ -605,6 +607,10 @@ void WaylandOutput::lockPointer(Pointer *pointer, bool lock)
         updateWindowTitle();
         Q_EMIT m_backend->pointerLockChanged(false);
     });
+    // A lock (its region) is double-buffered surface state: the host applies it at the surface's next commit.
+    // An idle nested session commits nothing - pointer motion only moves the cursor - so the lock waited for
+    // whatever repainted next. Commit now (the layers have no cached state between frames).
+    m_surface->commit(KWayland::Client::Surface::CommitFlag::None);
 }
 
 void WaylandOutput::inhibitShortcuts(bool inhibit)
